window.docContents[201].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Classical-Wave-Equations--Why-Characteristics-Organize-Hyperbolic-Theory":{"contents":"For the wave equation  in one spatial dimension the\ncharacteristics are the lines , and the general solution\ndecomposes into left- and right-traveling waves. A point disturbance in higher dimensions\npropagates along expanding spheres, and the sharp Huygens principle (in odd\ndimensions ) says that the influence of a localized disturbance\npasses through each point in a thin shell rather than lingering forever.\n\nPhysically, the wave equation models vibrating strings (),\nmembranes (), and acoustic or electromagnetic waves (). The\ncharacteristic speed  (in our normalization) is the speed of sound or\nlight. The d'Alembert, Kirchhoff, and Poisson formulas give the\nexplicit solution in dimensions 1, 3, and 2 respectively. Evans's Section 2.4\ndevelops this material;  (Vol. II,\nCh. VI) give the classical geometric perspective.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves\u0009Classical Wave Equations","header":"7.2.3.1. Why Characteristics Organize Hyperbolic Theory","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Classical-Wave-Equations--Why-Characteristics-Organize-Hyperbolic-Theory"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Bounded-Domains-and-Improved-Regularity--Improved-Time-and-Spatial-Regularity--Energy-Estimates-and-Elliptic-Slices":{"contents":"The fundamental difficulty in parabolic regularity is that time and space\nplay asymmetric roles. The time derivative  lives naturally in the dual\nspace , which is two spatial derivatives below the energy\nspace . Improving from  to \n(a gain of one spatial derivative for the velocity) can be obtained by\ntesting the Galerkin equation with its own velocity. For an autonomous\nsymmetric principal form, the spatial term is an exact energy derivative.\nDifferentiating the equation itself is a later step, used to control\n and improve the regularity of .\n\nOnce  is established, the parabolic equation at almost every\ntime becomes a static elliptic problem:\n\n\n\nStandard elliptic regularity (Evans, 2010, §6.3;\n, Ch. 8) then lifts\n from  to . This\nexplains the parabolic regularity mechanism: time regularity feeds spatial\nregularity through the equation itself.\n\nThe full chain therefore reads:\n\n\n\nEach step uses a different tool: energy testing, weak compactness, or\nelliptic estimates. The Lions–Magenes framework (1972, Vol. I, Ch. 3) provides the abstract\nGelfand-triple language in which this composition is natural.\n\nSuppose , where  is bounded, autonomous,\nand symmetric, and the remainder satisfiesFor a supplied Galerkin trajectory  with pivot forcing\n, every  satisfiesThe constants are independent of the trial dimension. When the principal\nform is nonnegative, uniform bounds for the initial energy and the remaining\n term give the velocity estimate. The existence theorem\n uses the stronger assumption of\nprincipal coercivity.\n\nUse the Galerkin velocity as test vector. Symmetry and time independence of\n identify  with\n. The equation therefore controls\n plus this exact principal-energy derivative. Bound the\nlower-order term by\n and the forcing term by\n, absorb both velocity factors with Young's inequality,\nand integrate. The earlier energy estimate controls the remaining\n term uniformly in the trial dimension.\n\nThe two coercivity assumptions have different jobs. Coercivity of the\nfull form  supplies the basic  estimate and continuous\ndependence on the load. Coercivity of the principal form turns its energy\ninto a bound for the  norm. Together with stable initial projections,\nthese estimates bound the Galerkin velocities uniformly in .\n\nThe existence theorem also allows the symmetric principal form to vary\nabsolutely continuously in time. Its derivative contributes one extra term:\n. An operator-norm bound\n controls this term by\n, so the same argument still applies.\n\nLet  be strongly measurable almost everywhere and\nuniformly bounded in operator norm on . Assume  is absolutely\ncontinuous in operator norm,  is symmetric for every ,\nand its supplied almost-everywhere derivative is bounded by .\nFor one , require bothfor every  and . Suppose also, for all such times\nand every ,With trial spaces that approximate every vector of  and pivot projections\nuniformly bounded in , every\n and  admit an integrated weak solution\nwhose time derivative is represented by .\nAlmost everywhere,The theorem records quantitative  derivative and \nstate bounds. Here  is the pivot embedding. The autonomous\ncase takes  and .\n\nFirst construct Galerkin families for bounded continuous pivot loads.\nThe principal-energy calculation bounds their velocities in ;\nfor time-dependent , the additional coefficient is . Pass to a\ncommon weak limit and identify the pivot velocity with the weak derivative\nof the state. Finally, approximate an arbitrary Bochner load by bounded\ncontinuous loads. The estimates depend only on their  norms, and\nfull-form coercivity controls the differences, so both state and velocity\npass through the forcing-completion argument.\n\nFor the second stage, assume the full form  is autonomous; in the\nprincipal-part application, . For a dual load  with derivative\n, the formal equation for  has load .\nThe construction makes the differentiated Galerkin state equal to the\noriginal coefficient derivative almost everywhere and proves\n as an identity of Bochner classes.\nPassing this identity through a common weak limit identifies the\ndifferentiated limit with the weak derivative of the state.\n\nFor an autonomous form and an absolutely continuous load whose derivative\nbelongs to , every finite-dimensional Galerkin approximation\ncanonically produces an -forced approximation of the differentiated\nequation. Its state is the original coefficient derivative almost everywhere,\nand the Bochner identityis proved rather than supplied as a hypothesis.\n\nWrite the coefficient equation as . Define its\nvelocity directly by . Absolute continuity of  and\n makes  absolutely continuous, and differentiation gives\n almost everywhere. Its initial value is\n. Thus the original right-hand side itself supplies the\ndifferentiated trajectory and agrees almost everywhere with .\nThe  load-derivative hypothesis gives the required\nfinite-dimensional  bounds. Applying the pivot inclusion identifies\nthe resulting Bochner classes: .\n\nThe differentiated Galerkin system starts from the velocity defined by\nthe original right-hand side at . The energy argument needs these\ninitial velocities to be bounded in  uniformly in . Choose\n to be the Ritz projection of ; its defining identity then\nreplaces the form term at the initial time by  for every trial\nvector . A pivot-valued compatibility residual gives the uniform bound.\n\nFor each finite-dimensional trial space, Lax–Milgram constructs the\nRitz projection for a bounded autonomous coercive form . It satisfies\nGalerkin orthogonality , Céa's estimate, and convergence\nalong an approximating family of trial spaces. Symmetry is not needed for\nthese statements.Suppose a vector  represents the initial residual:With , the initial value of the differentiated trajectory is\nthe pivot projection of that vector:Here  denotes the orthogonal projection in  onto .\n\nLax–Milgram defines the Ritz projection  by\n for every trial vector. Evaluate the original\nright-hand side at the initial time with . The Ritz\nidentity replaces the form term by , and compatibility leaves\n. This is precisely the\ncharacterizing equation for the pivot-orthogonal projection of\n onto .\n\nWith both families constructed, the limit must retain their\nfinite-dimensional relation. The formal proof extracts a weakly convergent\nultrafilter for the state, then refines that same filter for the velocity\nand differentiated state. All three limits therefore see the identity\n, which yields .\nThe differentiated load is only assumed square integrable, so its energy\nestimate depends on its  norm alone.\n\nFor a bounded autonomous coercive form , suppose the original and\ndifferentiated Galerkin families are supplied with initial pivot data\nconverging in  and uniformly bounded there, a uniform \nvelocity bound, and the Bochner identity\n. Assume the supplied original family\nalso carries its common integrated Galerkin energy bound. Both dual-valued\nloads  must lie in .One common weak compactness filter identifies the differentiated state with\nthe time derivative of the original limit and givesFor a pivot load with , take\n. The second derivative is dual-valued, while the\nidentified first derivative belongs to .\n\nApply the coercive energy estimate directly to each supplied\ndifferentiated family, using the  norm of its load. Extract\ncommon weak limits for the original state, its pivot velocity, and the\ndifferentiated state. Testing their Bochner identity with bounded linear\nfunctionals identifies the two limits for the first derivative. Passing\nthe differentiated weak equation to the limit gives\n; boundedness of  puts this residual\nin .\n\nCombining the Ritz initialization with the paired limit removes the\ndifferentiated family from the hypotheses. Given the original\nRitz-initialized family and the uniform velocity bound of the first stage,\nthe theorem constructs the differentiated approximations, their initial\nvalues, and the bounds that the paired compactness theorem requires; the\ncompatibility identity is stated once, for the ambient datum  and the\nresidual . This closes the second stage.\n\nFor a bounded autonomous coercive form , take an absolutely continuous\nload with  derivative and a pivot-valued initial compatibility\nresidual as in . Initialize the\nsupplied original Galerkin family, with its common integrated energy bound, by\nRitz projections of  and assume\nits uniform  velocity bound. The Ritz variational identity converts\nthe compatibility condition at the initial time into the exact pivot projection\nof the initial velocity. The paired  compactness theorem then constructs\nthe original solution and its differentiated trajectory on one common limit,\nwithIn the pivot-forcing specialization,  with\n supplies the differentiated load bound.\nIts pointwise size need not be bounded.\n\nInitialize each original Galerkin trajectory with the Ritz projection of\n. Ritz compatibility turns the assumed initial residual into a uniformly\ncontrolled pivot projection, which is valid initial data for the\ndifferentiated Galerkin system. Apply the paired  theorem to these two\nfamilies. Its common limit identifies the pivot derivative of  with\n and passes the differentiated equation to\n.\n\nThe third stage is spatial and, at first, entirely static. Once\n is known at a fixed time, the parabolic equation at\nthat time is an elliptic Dirichlet problem for  with right-hand side\n, and the question becomes global elliptic\n regularity for the principal part. No time regularity beyond the\npivot-valued derivative is used.\n\nAt a time where , , and\nthe weak residual identity holds, rewrite the parabolic equation asOn a domain equipped with a twice differentiable boundary atlas, assume the\nprincipal coefficients belong to  and have global\n representatives that are uniformly elliptic on .\nElliptic regularity then gives an  representative with the\nsame spatial value class as the time slice. The atlas is a hypothesis. For\na bounded open set with  graph boundary,\nthe graph-boundary atlas construction provides one.\n\nMove the pivot time-derivative term to the forcing side of the weak equation.\nThe resulting identity is exactly the elliptic Dirichlet weak problem with\nright-hand side . Apply the global\n regularity theorem for the operational  atlas and the\ncoefficient hypotheses of the statement. That theorem returns an  element whose\norder-zero representative agrees almost everywhere with the original\n time slice.\n\nApplying the slice theorem at almost every time gives a family of \nrepresentatives, but a family of slices is not yet an element of\n. Measurability in time comes from applying the\nbounded elliptic solution operator to the Bochner class , so that\nthe lift is the composition of a continuous linear map with a measurable\ntrajectory rather than an arbitrary choice at each time.\n\nRetain the domain and coefficient hypotheses of the slice theorem.\nIf , the weak evolution identity holds almost\neverywhere, and , there is a canonical\nBochner liftwhose canonical restriction to  agrees almost everywhere with\n. It satisfies the integrated elliptic estimatewhere  is the bounded global elliptic solution operator.\n\nApply the bounded elliptic  solution operator to the measurable residual\nf-u_t. Its  restriction agrees almost everywhere with the weak solution\nby elliptic uniqueness, and the operator norm gives the integrated estimate.\n\nThe first and third stages combine as soon as the time derivative is placed\non the forcing side of the equation. For an autonomous symmetric principal\noperator, the weak solution comes with  and an\nalmost-everywhere residual identity, which is exactly the hypothesis of the\nBochner lift; the second stage is not needed at this level of regularity.\n\nFor autonomous symmetric uniformly elliptic principal coefficients in\n with global  representatives, on a domain\ncarrying the twice differentiable boundary atlas, forcing in\n, and initial data in , there existThe theorem simultaneously returns the integrated weak equation, the\nalmost-everywhere pivot-valued residual, the identification of  with\n, and quantitative bounds for both  and . The abstract\nGalerkin theorem also records the explicit almost-everywhere energy-space\nnorm bound used to obtain the  membership.\n\nConstruct the autonomous weak solution with the improved pivot-valued time\nderivative, rewrite the equation at almost every time as an elliptic problem,\nand apply the Bochner-valued spatial  lift to .\n\nThe bootstrap bounds  by , so a solution quantity still\nappears on the right. Obtaining bounds in terms of the data alone means\nfeeding the first-stage velocity bound,\nwhich involves only , , and the form constants, into the elliptic\nestimate.\n\nUnder the hypotheses of , let  bound\nthe form and let  be a coercivity constant. SetThe constructed solution and its  lift satisfyThese are data-only bounds for the principal-part problem.\nOnce the domain, coefficients, and interval are\nfixed, their right-hand sides contain only the initial datum and forcing.\n\nThe improved Galerkin estimate bounds both the essential supremum of the\nprincipal energy and  solely by the initial energy,\n, and the form constants . The elliptic solution\noperator applied to  gives\n. Substituting the\nfirst data-only estimate into this inequality removes every solution\nquantity from the right-hand sides.\n\nStronger data give one more derivative in each direction, and here the\nsecond stage enters. With\n and ,\nthe Ritz-initialized paired construction identifies the differentiated\ntrajectory with , which therefore inherits an\n bound and a weak time derivative\n. Since  is now essentially\nbounded in , the elliptic slice estimate upgrades the\n lift to .\n\nRetain the operational domain and autonomous symmetric uniformly elliptic\n coefficient hypotheses, including the global \nrepresentatives. For initial data in  and forcing in\n, the paired Ritz–Galerkin construction identifies\nthe differentiated trajectory with the weak time derivative of the original\ntrajectory. Combining the differentiated energy and elliptic estimates givesandThe assumed time-Sobolev regularity\n supplies its own\n bound through the closed-interval\nLions–Magenes representative; no separate pointwise forcing bound is an\ninput. More precisely, the constructed representative satisfieson the closed interval, and the original Bochner trajectory satisfies the\nsame estimate almost everywhere.The estimate theorem records data-dependent bounds for the\n, , , and\n components displayed, including the quantitative bound for\n. The estimates control the second time derivative in\n.\n\nThe proof has four steps.Step 1: Differentiated energy. The forcing hypothesis\n implies . The\nRitz-compatible initial data give a differentiated Galerkin family whose\nenergy estimate bounds  and\n by the initial compatibility residual and\n.Step 2: Derivative identification. The paired compactness theorem\nextracts a common weak limit for both the original and differentiated\nGalerkin families. The finite-dimensional identity\n passes to the limit, proving that the\ndifferentiated weak solution is the time derivative of the original rather\nthan an independently selected function.Step 3: Second time derivative. From the weak equation\n (almost everywhere in time), differentiate in time:\n. Since  is bounded and\n, this gives .Step 4: Spatial bootstrap. At almost every time,  and\nthe equation gives .\nThe elliptic  theorem uses the operational  atlas and the\n coefficients with global  representatives. It lifts\n to . The differentiated energy estimate bounds\n in , and the time-Sobolev representative bounds\n in the same space. Their difference is therefore essentially bounded\nin ; applying the bounded elliptic solution operator gives\n (Evans, 2010, §7.1.3).\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Bounded Domains and Improved Regularity\u0009Improved Time and Spatial Regularity","header":"6.4.3.1. Energy Estimates and Elliptic Slices","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Bounded-Domains-and-Improved-Regularity--Improved-Time-and-Spatial-Regularity--Energy-Estimates-and-Elliptic-Slices"}});